#
\title{Virtual Address Translation (RISC-V Sv39)}
\maketitle
\section{Virtual Address Translation (RISC-V Sv39)}

# RISC-V Paging Hardware (review)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.18]{figures/ch03-fig-3-2-riscv-address-translation}
\label{fig:ch03-fig-3-2-riscv-address-translation}
\caption{RISC-V address translation details \parencite[pg.33]{cox-kaashoek-morris-2026}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=60%}
- RISC-V instructions (both user and kernel) manipulate virtual addresses
- The machine's RAM, or physical memory, is indexed with physical addresses
- The RISC-V page table hardware maps each virtual address to physical address
- Xv6 uses RISC-V Sv39 mode
  - only bottom 39 bits (of 64) are used
  - RISC-V page table is array of $2^{27} (134,217,728)$ **page table entries** PTEs
  - Each PTE contains 44-bit physical page number PPN and flags
  - Paging hardware translates top 27 bits of virtual address into page table
    index to find PTE
  - Results in 56-bit physical address, 44-bits from PPN, and 12-bit offset
::::
:::

# RISC-V Paging Hardware (review 2)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.18]{figures/ch03-fig-3-2-riscv-address-translation}
\label{fig:ch03-fig-3-2-riscv-address-translation}
\caption{RISC-V address translation details \parencite[pg.33]{cox-kaashoek-morris-2026}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=60%}
- A page table gives the operating system control over virtual-to-physical
  address translations
  - page size $2^{12} = 4096$ bytes `kernel/riscv.h` `PGSIZE`
  - Such a chunk is called a **page**
- Actual page table layout is a bit more complex, three-level tree
  - root of tree is 4096-byte page table containing 512 PTEs (8 bytes or 64 bits
    per PTE)
  - first 9 bits of virtual address index top level page table, next mid level,
    etc.
    - Allows memory-efficient way of managing PTEs
  - Potentially requires 3 memory accesses for each VA $\rightarrow$ PA
    translation
    - RISC-V CPU uses **Translation Look-aside Buffer** (TLB)
::::
:::

# RISC-V Paging Hardware (review 3)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.18]{figures/ch03-fig-3-2-riscv-address-translation}
\label{fig:ch03-fig-3-2-riscv-address-translation}
\caption{RISC-V address translation details \parencite[pg.33]{cox-kaashoek-morris-2026}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=60%}
- PTE_V Valid, indicates whether the PTE is present, if not reference causes
  page fault
- PTE_R Readable, controls whether instructions are allowed to read the page
- PTE_W Writable, controls whether instructions are allowed to write to the page
- PTE_X Executable, controls whether CPU may interpret content of page as
  instructions and execute them
- PTE_U User, controls whether instructions in user mode are allowed to access
  page
- PTE_A Accessed, indicates if page has ever been accessed (read from) since
  created
- PTE_D Dirty, indicates if page has been written to
::::
:::

# RISC-V Virtual Address Translation Question

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- Assume following table shows page directory that maps to va 0x0000000000
- Assume commands are being run in user mode, unless otherwise specified
- Just given the physical page number, and flags (the 8 used flag bits)
- Then given a virtual address, determine if a fault occurs (and if so what
  type) or if not, what the actual physical address is.

Examples

1. VA 0x31FA (load from address to register)
2. VA 0x1010 (fetch instruction user mode)
3. VA 0x61A0 (load from address to register)
4. VA 0x00F0 (load from address to register)  
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
| Page | PPN (44) | Flags (10) |
| ---- | -------- | ---------- |
| 6    | 0x005F   | 0x72       |
| 5    | 0x0101   |            |
| 4    | 0x000A   |            |
| 3    | 0x223F   | 0x73       |
| 2    | 0x0007   |            |
| 1    | 0x001F   | 0x7B       |
| 0    | 0x3FAF   | 0x63       |
::::
:::

# 
\title{Demand Paging and Page Eviction Algorithms}
\maketitle
\section{Demand Paging and Page Eviction Algorithms}


# Demand Paging to Disk and Page Eviction Algorithms

- Demand paging is often paired with paging to disk to provide unlimited virtual
  address space.
- Pages can be in secondary storage, PTE_V will be unset (invalid)
- Steady state in many environments is for memory to be full, so if a page fault
  occurs, need to select a page in memory to be evicted
  - Possibly need to write out first if dirty
- Page Eviction algorithms can have huge impact on performance, since eviction
  is costly
  - Want to select pages to evict that minimize page faults

# Page Eviction Algorithms

- FIFO: treat memory as a first-in-first out queue, evict page that was in
  memory longest
  - Worst performance, will cause most page faults to occur of many eviction
    algorithms
  - Question: is xv6 memory allocation FIFO? Could it be easily turned into
    FIFO?
- LRU: least recently used, determine which physical frame hasn't been used for
  longest time
  - Best performance, but high overhead
  - e.g. How would we modify the xv6 memory allocator to support LRU?
- Optimal: pick page that won't be used for the longest amount of time in the
  future
  - This is only for simulations, we can't actually predict the future
- Clock: Modified FIFO, but use the access / use bit to try and select pages not
  recently used
  - This is strategy most modern operating systems use
  - The RISC-V Dirty and Accessed bits can be used for a clock eviction
    algorithm

# Page Eviction Algorithms (FIFO, LRU, Optimal)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- Assume we have a physical memory with only 3 physical pages available (called
  physical frames)
- Given a stream of page references, show result of physical memory after each
  page reference


|          | 0   | 1   | 2   | 3   | 4   | 5   |
| -------- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Frame 1: | 2   |     |     |     |     |     |
| Frame 2: | 1   |     |     |     |     |     |
| Frame 3: | 3   |     |     |     |     |     |

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
Example page reference stream:

| T1  | 2   | 3   | 4   | 5   |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 4   | 2   | 1   | 4   | 2   |
|     |     |     |     |     |
::::
:::

# Clock Page Eviction Algorithms

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
1. Beginning at current frame pointer, scan until we find a frame with (a=0,
   d=0)
2. If a scan is made and no page meets (1), do a second scan for a page with
   (a=0, d=1). During this scan each access bit that is 1 is set to 0.
3. If step (2) fails, the frame pointer has returned to the initial frame.
   Repeat step (1) and if necessary step (2).

- Frame pointer always points to frame after last one evicted.
- When new page is loaded, a=1
- Hardware sets a=1 on read/write
- If a new page is loaded for write, d=1
- Hardware sets d=1 on writes

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
Example page reference stream:

| T1    | 2     | 3     | 4     | 5     |
| ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- |
| 4 (r) | 2 (w) | 1 (w) | 4 (r) | 2 (w) |
|       |       |       |       |       |

\tiny
|     | 0         | 1               | 2               | 3               | 4               | 5               |
| --- | --------- | --------------- | --------------- | --------------- | --------------- | --------------- |
| F1: | 2 a=1,d=0 | \hspace{0.02in} | \hspace{0.02in} | \hspace{0.02in} | \hspace{0.02in} | \hspace{0.02in} |
| F2: | 1 a=0,d=0 |                 |                 |                 |                 |                 |
| F3: | 3 a=0,d=1 |                 |                 |                 |                 |                 |
::::
:::


